 "batch_notes": "Batch dominated by codec/parser targets, so roundtrip and external-oracle lead. Two targets (python-pkginfo, r-description) follow the 'Pinned (target/N)' convention: many generative properties that exist only to pin one bug and have the bug id in their doc; all tagged pin, and since the bugs already carry property attributions no attribution entries were needed for them. qoi and rasn have KNOWN FAILURE properties with empty bugs_direct (prop_decode_3ch_uninit_index_diverges_from_c_reference, bug_oid_second_arc_overflow) that are second manifestations of bugs already linked to a sibling property; tagged pin but they have no bug record of their own to attribute. Recurring shape the rubric handles awkwardly: 'never panics AND on success some conservation/bound holds' (partial_decode_accounts_for_every_byte, varint_decode_arbitrary_bytes, prop_read_varint_arbitrary_bytes, prop_decode_garbage_body_never_panics); I split by what the name/doc leads with, so identical shapes land in robustness or spec-postcondition. Another recurring shape: 'decode with conversion equals input transformed by a trivial test-side map' (qoi rgb<->rgba) and 'DER fixpoint' (rasn) sit between roundtrip, spec-postcondition and algebraic-law; classified spec-postcondition and algebraic-law respectively. Random-op sequences with invariants but no reference model (rangemap full_i32_domain, quorum-set monotone_under_resets) were put in stateful-model per the 'or invariants' clause. Quality: quick-xml normalize_*_eols, quinn cid_queue and all radix_trie/rangemap/rasn properties have no doc comments (README says '(no doc comment)'), so classification relied on bodies; rasn's model_roundtrip_all_codecs encodes each value twice per codec, harmless but sloppy."
